//
// THE FONT — the ONE seam between dryopea and `graphics::draw_text`.
//
// #examples
//
// BACKLOG B1.  ⚠⚠ **dryopea rasterises nothing here.**  `graphics`
// already owns the glyph work (`load_font` / `Font.measure` /
// `Font.line_height` / `Canvas.draw_text`), so what this file owns is
// the three things `graphics` cannot know: **WHICH font, WHERE it
// lives, and what a font that did not load is allowed to do.**
//
// ── The invariant ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **dryopea owns the font's ABSOLUTE path and loads it once; every
// draw goes through one door that has already proven the face
// loaded — so neither the caller's cwd nor the entry's directory can
// decide which typeface appears, and a font that did not load REFUSES
// BY NAME instead of drawing nothing without a word.**
//
// ── Why that sentence has three clauses ─────────────────────────
//
// Each one is a MEASURED failure, not a precaution (`@M047`).
//
// ⚠⚠ **A FONT THAT DID NOT LOAD IS A NULL, AND THE SEAM IS WHAT
// GIVES IT A NAME.**  `graphics::load_font` answers `Font?`: null for
// a missing file and for a file that is not a TrueType font, and
// nothing in `graphics` takes a null face — the compiler refuses the
// call.  ⚠ That closed the trap this file was first written against
// (on `graphics` 0.9 a font was an integer handle, a failed load
// collapsed to handle 0, and text was drawn silently in whatever font
// had loaded FIRST).  What is left for the seam is the half the type
// cannot do: say WHICH file was asked for and WHY it is not a font
// (`text_fault`), and answer whether a draw happened (`text_draw`).
// **That is why `TextFont` carries `path` and `loaded` and every door
// asks.**
//
// ⚠ It is `TextFont` and not `Font` because `graphics` 0.10 took the
// name `Font` for the face itself, and this file imports `graphics`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A RELATIVE PATH DOES NOT MEAN THE REPO ROOT.**
// `graphics::load_font` joins a relative path to `source_dir()` — the
// ENTRY's directory — so `assets/…` asked from a test is
// `tests/assets/…` and loads NOTHING, while `is_file("assets/…")`
// under `#cwd` says the file is there.  Two answers for one path is
// the failure, so `text_font_path` builds an **absolute** one, which
// is passed through verbatim, and `font_load_from` refuses any other.
//
// ⚠ **`#cwd` does NOT move `source_dir()`** — measured: under
// `loft test` it still answers `<repo>/tests`.  The two mechanisms
// are unrelated, so dryopea's `#cwd` + repo-root-relative rule (which
// governs `file()`, and therefore `examples/palette.json`) says
// nothing about where a font is found.
//
// ── Why the path is built from `source_dir()` ───────────────────
//
// ⚠ It is the only ABSOLUTE anchor pointing into the repo that loft
// offers: there is no `cwd()` builtin, and `program_directory()` is
// the loft binary's.  Every dryopea entry — `src/main.loft`,
// `src/validate_main.loft`, `src/gl_gate_main.loft`,
// `src/mapbuild_main.loft` and every `tests/*.loft` — sits exactly
// ONE level under the repo root, so `{source_dir()}/../assets` is the
// root's `assets/` from all of them.  ⚠⚠ **That is an assumption
// about the tree's SHAPE, so it is not left to a comment**:
// `text_fault` asks `is_file` and NAMES the path it tried, and
// `tests/b1_the_text_seam.loft` fails on the name rather than on a
// blank canvas.  A nested entry directory would go red at once.
//
// ── What this seam does NOT decide ──────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ **Anti-aliased text is not a palette colour, and the number is
// large** (`@M047`): one 18 px line of white-on-black measures 1324
// lit pixels of which only **550 are pure white** — **774 are
// blended**.  `measure.loft::classify_canvas` looks colours up
// EXACTLY, so every one of those 774 lands in `unknown`, and both
// `@X077`'s `other == 0` and `@X092`'s `unknown - entity pixels == 0`
// are assertions text would break.
//
// ⚠ **That is the first CONSUMER's problem and it is deliberately
// left open here.**  The precedent is already in the tree and it is
// `@M042`'s: the wallet overlay is composed on its OWN canvas and
// blitted 1:1, so the gate counts the ink in that canvas rather than
// hunting it in the frame.  A door that thresholds text to one flat
// colour is the other candidate — cheap to add over `text_draw`, and
// **not added now, because a door with no consumer is a door nobody
// has measured**.  ⚠ What must NOT happen is loosening
// `classify_canvas` to a nearest-colour match: `docs/RENDERER.md`
// § R0 measured exact classification through GL at zero drift, and
// that property is what three gates stand on.

use graphics::*;

// The font, relative to the repo root.  ⚠ DejaVu Sans **Bold**:
// BOLD because text is drawn over a busy world and a hairline face is
// unreadable there, and DejaVu because its Bitstream Vera licence is
// permissive — `assets/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt` travels with it, as that
// licence requires.  ⚠ Liberation Sans Narrow (which `graphics`
// itself bundles) was refused on licence rather than on looks: it is
// GPL-2-with-a-font-exception, which is copyleft, and this repo is
// LGPL-3.0-or-later.
pub const TEXT_FONT_FILE: text = "assets/DejaVuSans-Bold.ttf";

// A loaded font.
//
// ── Why there are TWO fields for one fact ───────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ `loaded` looks redundant beside a null `face`, and the repo's
// own rule is against it — `carry.loft` refuses a *carried* flag
// beside an owner because **two facts can disagree**.  It is here
// anyway, and the reason is [loft#914]: **a struct literal that omits
// a field takes that field's default SILENTLY.**  Both fields fall
// the safe way (an omitted `face` is null, an omitted `loaded` is
// false), so a partial literal FAILS CLOSED whichever one it forgets.
// ⚠ The two can only disagree through a hand-built literal, because
// `font_load_from` is the only constructor of a loaded `TextFont` and
// it sets both at one site.
//
// ⚠⚠ **The doors read `loaded` FIRST and then need the face** — and
// the two stop DIFFERENT failures, which is measured rather than
// assumed (`tests/b1_the_font_seam.loft`).  A *missing file* has no
// face, so nothing could be drawn anyway: what `loaded` guards there
// is `text_draw`'s ANSWER.  What it stops in PIXELS is the other case
// — a `TextFont` carrying a LIVE face with `loaded` false, which is
// what a hand-built literal makes.
pub struct TextFont {
    pub face:   Font?,
    pub path:   text,
    pub loaded: boolean,
}

// Where the repo's font is, as an ABSOLUTE path.
//
// ⚠⚠ Absolute is the requirement, not a convenience: it is the one
// form `is_file` and `graphics::load_font` agree on (§ A RELATIVE PATH
// DOES NOT MEAN THE REPO ROOT).  ⚠ It is a function and not a `const`
// because `source_dir()` is `#impure(host_io)` and loft refuses a
// call in a const initialiser.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_font_path() -> text {
    "{source_dir()}/../{TEXT_FONT_FILE}"
}

// A `TextFont` that never loaded — what a caller holds before asking, and
// what `font_load_from` answers on any failure.
//
// Example: @DRY-097
pub fn font_missing() -> TextFont {
    TextFont { face: null, path: "", loaded: false }
}

// Load a font from an ABSOLUTE path.  ⚠ The door for a test, or for a
// future setting that names a face; `font_load` is the one the game
// calls.
//
// ⚠ It asks `is_file` FIRST so the two failures stay apart: *the file
// is not there* and *the file is there and the loader refused it* are
// different problems with different fixes, and one message covering
// both would name neither.
//
// Example: @DRY-097
pub fn font_load_from(flf_path: text) -> TextFont {
    // ⚠⚠ ABSOLUTE is ENFORCED, not just documented.  A relative path
    // would pass `is_file` (cwd-relative) and then mean something
    // ELSE to `load_font` (the entry's directory) — which is the
    // whole failure this file exists to prevent, and a public door
    // that accepts one silently is where it would come back in.
    if !flf_path.starts_with("/") {
        return TextFont { face: null, path: flf_path, loaded: false };
    }
    if !is_file(flf_path) {
        return TextFont { face: null, path: flf_path, loaded: false };
    }
    flf_face = graphics::load_font(flf_path);
    if flf_face == null {
        return TextFont { face: null, path: flf_path, loaded: false };
    }
    TextFont { face: flf_face, path: flf_path, loaded: true }
}

// Load the repo's own font.  ⚠ The ONE call the game makes, and it
// takes no argument on purpose: a path parameter here is a second
// place the answer to *which font* could live.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn font_load() -> TextFont {
    font_load_from(text_font_path())
}

// May this font be drawn with?  ⚠⚠ **Every door below asks this, and
// nothing outside this file should have to** — that is what makes the
// seam a chokepoint rather than a convention.
//
// Example: none — a one-line accessor over `f.loaded` and `f.face`
pub fn font_ready(fr_f: const TextFont) -> boolean {
    fr_f.loaded && fr_f.face != null
}

// What is wrong with this font — `""` when nothing is.
//
// ⚠ It NAMES the path, because the failure this file exists to
// prevent is a silent one: a blank corner and a corner drawn in the
// wrong face look identical, and neither says where it looked.
//
// Example: @DRY-097
pub fn text_fault(tf_f: const TextFont) -> text {
    if font_ready(tf_f) { return ""; }
    if tf_f.path == "" { return "no font has been loaded"; }
    if !tf_f.path.starts_with("/") {
        return "`{tf_f.path}` is a RELATIVE font path — `graphics` would look for it beside the entry file and not in the repo, so only an absolute one is accepted";
    }
    if !is_file(tf_f.path) {
        return "no font file at `{tf_f.path}` — `{TEXT_FONT_FILE}` is missing from the repo";
    }
    "`{tf_f.path}` exists but `graphics::load_font` refused it — not a font this build can read"
}

// How wide `tw_s` draws, in pixels.  0 for a font that did not load.
//
// ⚠ It rounds the way `graphics` sizes the line it rasterises
// (`as integer + 1`) rather than independently, so a caller that
// reserves this many pixels reserves what the ink will occupy.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_width(tw_f: const TextFont, tw_s: text, tw_size: float) -> integer {
    if !tw_f.loaded { return 0; }
    tw_face = tw_f.face ?? return 0;
    tw_face.measure(tw_s, tw_size) as integer + 1
}

// One line's height in pixels.  0 for a font that did not load.
//
// ⚠ 0 is what a caller laying out lines can add up without asking
// first; whether a font is there is `font_ready`'s question and why
// it is not is `text_fault`'s.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_line_height(tlh_f: const TextFont, tlh_size: float) -> integer {
    if !tlh_f.loaded { return 0; }
    tlh_face = tlh_f.face ?? return 0;
    tlh_face.line_height(tlh_size)
}

// Draw `td_s` at `(td_x, td_y)`, the TOP-left of the line.  Answers
// whether anything was drawn.
//
// ⚠⚠ **This is the only place in dryopea that may call
// `graphics`' `draw_text`.**  A second call site is a second place
// the `loaded` check can be forgotten, and a second answer to *was
// anything drawn*.
//
// ⚠ The boolean is what makes the refusal VISIBLE to a test: a draw
// that silently does nothing and a draw that succeeded look the same
// from the outside, and `tests/b1_the_text_seam.loft` asserts on this
// as well as on the pixels.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_draw(td_cv: Canvas, td_f: const TextFont, td_s: text, td_size: float,
                 td_x: integer, td_y: integer, td_colour: integer) -> boolean {
    if !td_f.loaded { return false; }
    td_face = td_f.face ?? return false;
    if td_s == "" { return false; }
    td_cv.draw_text(td_face, td_s, td_size, td_x, td_y, td_colour);
    true
}
